{"parentUuid":"545313af-fd4c-47a2-9dc4-6b2766974f88","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"resp_0b4e33ff6ca3e849016a7a53bbac108191942b21a88c8c2edb","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"codex-gpt-5-6-sol","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1693,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":60928,"output_tokens":119,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_f5LPOhfeDT4ryRoUPBke5HJP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\answer.ts","offset":1860,"limit":260}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_details":null},"attributionSkill":"simplify","type":"assistant","uuid":"db573407-acf3-4613-88d9-f820e7b14f22","timestamp":"2026-08-10T22:42:09.708Z","effort":"high","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.226","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"db573407-acf3-4613-88d9-f820e7b14f22","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0a8de032-5fd3-4106-bcf4-797ed6a2efc2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_f5LPOhfeDT4ryRoUPBke5HJP","type":"tool_result","content":"1860\t * pattern to the window start is exactly \"the line starts with this row\".\n1861\t *\n1862\t * Returns ALL the indices rather than a boolean, because the caller then has to\n1863\t * choose a placement per row that satisfies `rowsFormPickerBlock` — \"each needle\n1864\t * is somewhere on screen\" is a much weaker claim than \"these rows are rendered\n1865\t * as one list\", and a row's digit can legitimately appear more than once (an\n1866\t * N-question prompt restarts its numbering for every question).\n1867\t */\n1868\tfunction screenRowWindows(\n1869\t\twindows: readonly string[],\n1870\t\toptionIndex: number,\n1871\t\tlabel: string,\n1872\t): number[] {\n1873\t\tconst anchor = anchorOf(label, SCREEN_OPTION_ANCHOR_CHARS);\n1874\t\tif (anchor.length === 0) return [];\n1875\t\tconst digit = optionIndex + 1;\n1876\t\tconst pattern = new RegExp(\n1877\t\t\t`^[^0-9a-z]{0,${SCREEN_ROW_DECORATION_MAX_CHARS}}${digit}[^a-z0-9]{0,${SCREEN_ROW_DECORATION_MAX_CHARS}}${escapeRegExp(anchor)}`,\n1878\t\t);\n1879\t\tconst hits: number[] = [];\n1880\t\tfor (let i = 0; i < windows.length; i += 1) {\n1881\t\t\tconst window = windows[i];\n1882\t\t\tif (window !== undefined && pattern.test(window)) hits.push(i);\n1883\t\t}\n1884\t\treturn hits;\n1885\t}\n1886\t\n1887\t/**\n1888\t * (GUARD5-ANCHOR / PICKER-CHROME) Does the viewport show the STRUCTURE of a\n1889\t * picker — a run of numbered rows in ascending digit order starting at 1?\n1890\t *\n1891\t * Nothing in this function comes from a capture: it is the only screen evidence\n1892\t * the bridge has that no caller can parameterise. It is deliberately used ONLY\n1893\t * where a false positive is a refusal:\n1894\t *   - `/v1/message`, where a picker on screen must block the trailing `\\r`.\n1895\t * It is NOT used to permit anything.\n1896\t *\n1897\t * IT ERRS TOWARD MATCHING, deliberately and asymmetrically. A false positive\n1898\t * costs one refused message, which the user retries or types at the desk. A\n1899\t * false NEGATIVE writes a bare `\\r` into an open picker, which toggles a\n1900\t * multi-select row or submits an N-question review screen irreversibly. So the\n1901\t * two bounds that used to make this miss ordinary renders are gone:\n1902\t *\n1903\t *  - there is NO cap on the distance between consecutive rows. A cap of two\n1904\t *    lines was measured to miss a picker whose first option label WRAPPED across\n1905\t *    three lines, and a picker with two blank lines between rows. Ascending\n1906\t *    digit order across the whole viewport is the structure being looked for;\n1907\t *    the gap was never part of it.\n1908\t *  - leading decoration is unbounded (`[^0-9A-Za-z]*`) rather than capped at\n1909\t *    eight characters, so box drawing plus a selection caret plus deep\n1910\t *    indentation cannot push the digit out of reach. The character class still\n1911\t *    cannot cross a letter or another digit, so this does not match mid-line\n1912\t *    numbers.\n1913\t *\n1914\t * An agent that printed an ordinary numbered list still matches, and that is\n1915\t * still the correct trade.\n1916\t */\n1917\texport const PICKER_CHROME_MIN_ROWS = 2;\n1918\tconst PICKER_CHROME_ROW = /^[^0-9A-Za-z]*([1-9])[.)\\]:]?\\s+\\S/;\n1919\t\n1920\texport function screenShowsPickerChrome(screen: string): boolean {\n1921\t\tconst lines = screen.split(\"\\n\");\n1922\t\t/** Which digit the run expects next. */\n1923\t\tlet expected = 1;\n1924\t\tlet run = 0;\n1925\t\tfor (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n1926\t\t\tconst line = lines[i];\n1927\t\t\tif (line === undefined) continue;\n1928\t\t\tconst match = PICKER_CHROME_ROW.exec(line);\n1929\t\t\tif (match === null) continue;\n1930\t\t\tconst digit = Number(match[1]);\n1931\t\t\tif (run > 0 && digit === expected) {\n1932\t\t\t\trun += 1;\n1933\t\t\t\texpected += 1;\n1934\t\t\t\tif (run >= PICKER_CHROME_MIN_ROWS) return true;\n1935\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n1936\t\t\t}\n1937\t\t\tif (digit === 1) {\n1938\t\t\t\trun = 1;\n1939\t\t\t\texpected = 2;\n1940\t\t\t\tif (run >= PICKER_CHROME_MIN_ROWS) return true;\n1941\t\t\t}\n1942\t\t\t// A numbered line that neither continues the run nor starts one is IGNORED,\n1943\t\t\t// not treated as the end of the run. Resetting here was a third way to miss\n1944\t\t\t// a wrapped label, on top of the gap cap and the decoration cap: a\n1945\t\t\t// continuation line reading \"   3 tables and rebuilding every index\" matches\n1946\t\t\t// this row pattern with digit 3, and between rows 1 and 2 that reset dropped\n1947\t\t\t// the run and the picker went undetected. Skipping instead can only ever\n1948\t\t\t// ADD matches, which on this detector is the refusing direction.\n1949\t\t}\n1950\t\treturn false;\n1951\t}\n1952\t\n1953\t/**\n1954\t * The WEAK form, for the screens whose layout was never proven: the N-question\n1955\t * review screen, the multi-select Submit tab, the open free-text editor. It can\n1956\t * only assert that this prompt is still what is on screen. It is used ONLY for\n1957\t * `same_prompt` expectations and is reported as `digitMapped: false` so nothing\n1958\t * downstream can mistake it for the strong check.\n1959\t *\n1960\t * (GUARD5-ANCHOR) `anchor_too_weak` is reported separately from `anchor_absent`\n1961\t * because the two mean opposite things to a REFUSING caller: \"this prompt is\n1962\t * demonstrably not on screen\" versus \"this prompt has no anchor worth\n1963\t * searching for, so nothing was demonstrated\". A caller using this to rule a\n1964\t * picker OUT must treat `anchor_too_weak` as inconclusive.\n1965\t */\n1966\texport function matchPromptStillOnScreen(input: {\n1967\t\tscreen: string;\n1968\t\titem: QuestionItem;\n1969\t}): PickerScreenMatch {\n1970\t\tconst prologue = matchPromptOnScreen({\n1971\t\t\tscreen: input.screen,\n1972\t\t\titem: input.item,\n1973\t\t\tdigitMapped: false,\n1974\t\t});\n1975\t\tif (!prologue.ok) return prologue.failure;\n1976\t\t// Deliberately nothing else. The prompt being on screen is the WHOLE claim\n1977\t\t// this form makes; the option rows are not consulted at all.\n1978\t\treturn { ok: true, reason: \"match\", missing: [], digitMapped: false };\n1979\t}\n1980\t\n1981\tfunction evaluateScreenGuard(input: {\n1982\t\tscreen: string;\n1983\t\tquestion: PendingQuestion;\n1984\t\texpectation: ScreenExpectation;\n1985\t\trequireOptionIndex: number | null;\n1986\t}): PickerScreenMatch {\n1987\t\tconst item = input.question.questions[input.expectation.itemIndex];\n1988\t\tif (item === undefined) {\n1989\t\t\treturn {\n1990\t\t\t\tok: false,\n1991\t\t\t\treason: \"anchor_absent\",\n1992\t\t\t\tmissing: [`item:${input.expectation.itemIndex}`],\n1993\t\t\t\tdigitMapped: input.expectation.kind === \"item_picker\",\n1994\t\t\t};\n1995\t\t}\n1996\t\tif (input.expectation.kind === \"item_picker\") {\n1997\t\t\treturn matchPickerScreen({\n1998\t\t\t\tscreen: input.screen,\n1999\t\t\t\titem,\n2000\t\t\t\trequireOptionIndex: input.requireOptionIndex,\n2001\t\t\t});\n2002\t\t}\n2003\t\treturn matchPromptStillOnScreen({ screen: input.screen, item });\n2004\t}\n2005\t\n2006\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2007\t// the guard stack\n2008\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2009\t\n2010\texport interface GuardOutcome {\n2011\t\tevaluation: GuardEvaluation;\n2012\t\t/**\n2013\t\t * The guards that PASSED — the durable audit answer to \"what permitted this\n2014\t\t * write\". An abstaining guard is deliberately NOT here: it did not pass, and a\n2015\t\t * row saying it did while `evaluation` records `false` for it is a durable\n2016\t\t * self-contradiction nobody can later resolve.\n2017\t\t */\n2018\t\tpassed: AnswerGuardName[];\n2019\t\t/**\n2020\t\t * (GUARD4-ABSTAIN) Guards that did NOT read positively and were carried\n2021\t\t * anyway, because every load-bearing guard had passed and they are in\n2022\t\t * `ABSTAINING_GUARDS`. There is no expectation-form condition: the axis\n2023\t\t * abstains on weak `same_prompt` keystrokes too, or it aborts proven\n2024\t\t * free-text sequences mid-write. Disjoint from `passed`.\n2025\t\t */\n2026\t\tabstained: AnswerGuardName[];\n2027\t\t/** The FIRST guard that failed, in evaluation order. */\n2028\t\tfailed: AnswerGuardName | null;\n2029\t\tscreenMatch: PickerScreenMatch | null;\n2030\t}\n2031\t\n2032\t/** The attempt-wide guard evidence carried into the ledger, audit and wire. */\n2033\texport interface AttemptEvidence {\n2034\t\tguardsPassed: AnswerGuardName[];\n2035\t\tguardsAbstained: AnswerGuardName[];\n2036\t\tevaluation: GuardEvaluation;\n2037\t}\n2038\t\n2039\t/**\n2040\t * Fold one keystroke's `GuardOutcome` into the attempt-wide evidence record.\n2041\t *\n2042\t * The guards run before EVERY byte and their sources can change between reads\n2043\t * (the permission axis is a latch any hook event overwrites), so a record that\n2044\t * keeps only the final keystroke's picture can claim \"nothing abstained\" about\n2045\t * a sequence whose earlier bytes were permitted only by abstention — a false\n2046\t * ledger row, a false audit event, and a falsely-derived replay. So:\n2047\t *\n2048\t *   - `guardsAbstained` is the UNION across keystrokes: abstaining once is an\n2049\t *     attempt-wide fact;\n2050\t *   - `guardsPassed` is the INTERSECTION, minus anything that ever abstained —\n2051\t *     a guard passed the attempt only if it passed every evaluation;\n2052\t *   - `evaluation` ANDs per guard: the raw reading is `true` only if it read\n2053\t *     `true` before every byte.\n2054\t *\n2055\t * Disjointness (`guardsPassed` ∩ `guardsAbstained` = ∅) holds attempt-wide.\n2056\t * `previous` is null on the first keystroke.\n2057\t */\n2058\texport function mergeAttemptEvidence(\n2059\t\tprevious: AttemptEvidence | null,\n2060\t\toutcome: GuardOutcome,\n2061\t): AttemptEvidence {\n2062\t\tconst guardsAbstained =\n2063\t\t\tprevious === null\n2064\t\t\t\t? [...outcome.abstained]\n2065\t\t\t\t: [...new Set([...previous.guardsAbstained, ...outcome.abstained])];\n2066\t\tconst guardsPassed = (\n2067\t\t\tprevious === null\n2068\t\t\t\t? [...outcome.passed]\n2069\t\t\t\t: previous.guardsPassed.filter((guard) => outcome.passed.includes(guard))\n2070\t\t).filter((guard) => !guardsAbstained.includes(guard));\n2071\t\tconst evaluation =\n2072\t\t\tprevious === null\n2073\t\t\t\t? { ...outcome.evaluation }\n2074\t\t\t\t: (Object.fromEntries(\n2075\t\t\t\t\t\t(Object.keys(outcome.evaluation) as AnswerGuardName[]).map(\n2076\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(guard) => [\n2077\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tguard,\n2078\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tprevious.evaluation[guard] && outcome.evaluation[guard],\n2079\t\t\t\t\t\t\t],\n2080\t\t\t\t\t\t),\n2081\t\t\t\t\t) as GuardEvaluation);\n2082\t\treturn { guardsPassed, guardsAbstained, evaluation };\n2083\t}\n2084\t\n2085\t/** A source that throws or answers `null` counts as FALSE. Never \"probably fine\". */\n2086\tasync function readGuardSource(\n2087\t\tname: AnswerGuardName,\n2088\t\tdeps: AnswerDeps,\n2089\t\tread: () => Promise<GuardSourceResult>,\n2090\t): Promise<boolean> {\n2091\t\ttry {\n2092\t\t\tconst value = await read();\n2093\t\t\tif (value === null) {\n2094\t\t\t\tdeps.log({\n2095\t\t\t\t\tevent: \"companion.guard.indeterminate\",\n2096\t\t\t\t\tguard: name,\n2097\t\t\t\t\tresult: false,\n2098\t\t\t\t});\n2099\t\t\t\treturn false;\n2100\t\t\t}\n2101\t\t\treturn value;\n2102\t\t} catch (error) {\n2103\t\t\tdeps.log({\n2104\t\t\t\tevent: \"companion.guard.error\",\n2105\t\t\t\tguard: name,\n2106\t\t\t\tresult: false,\n2107\t\t\t\terror: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),\n2108\t\t\t});\n2109\t\t\treturn false;\n2110\t\t}\n2111\t}\n2112\t\n2113\t/**\n2114\t * The reader for a `veto_only` guard, which has DIFFERENT null semantics from\n2115\t * every other class and therefore cannot share `readGuardSource`.\n2116\t *\n2117\t * `GUARD_CLASSES` defines veto_only as \"Absence is a sound veto; presence is\n2118\t * never evidence\". Only an AFFIRMATIVE `false` — the source was read and the\n2119\t * thing is genuinely absent — may veto. `null` means the source could not be"}]},"uuid":"aaf1ce40-b26a-4fc5-a3e4-34cd4d9dc758","timestamp":"2026-08-10T22:42:10.333Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\answer.ts","content":" * pattern to the window start is exactly \"the line starts with this row\".\n *\n * Returns ALL the indices rather than a boolean, because the caller then has to\n * choose a placement per row that satisfies `rowsFormPickerBlock` — \"each needle\n * is somewhere on screen\" is a much weaker claim than \"these rows are rendered\n * as one list\", and a row's digit can legitimately appear more than once (an\n * N-question prompt restarts its numbering for every question).\n */\nfunction screenRowWindows(\n\twindows: readonly string[],\n\toptionIndex: number,\n\tlabel: string,\n): number[] {\n\tconst anchor = anchorOf(label, SCREEN_OPTION_ANCHOR_CHARS);\n\tif (anchor.length === 0) return [];\n\tconst digit = optionIndex + 1;\n\tconst pattern = new RegExp(\n\t\t`^[^0-9a-z]{0,${SCREEN_ROW_DECORATION_MAX_CHARS}}${digit}[^a-z0-9]{0,${SCREEN_ROW_DECORATION_MAX_CHARS}}${escapeRegExp(anchor)}`,\n\t);\n\tconst hits: number[] = [];\n\tfor (let i = 0; i < windows.length; i += 1) {\n\t\tconst window = windows[i];\n\t\tif (window !== undefined && pattern.test(window)) hits.push(i);\n\t}\n\treturn hits;\n}\n\n/**\n * (GUARD5-ANCHOR / PICKER-CHROME) Does the viewport show the STRUCTURE of a\n * picker — a run of numbered rows in ascending digit order starting at 1?\n *\n * Nothing in this function comes from a capture: it is the only screen evidence\n * the bridge has that no caller can parameterise. It is deliberately used ONLY\n * where a false positive is a refusal:\n *   - `/v1/message`, where a picker on screen must block the trailing `\\r`.\n * It is NOT used to permit anything.\n *\n * IT ERRS TOWARD MATCHING, deliberately and asymmetrically. A false positive\n * costs one refused message, which the user retries or types at the desk. A\n * false NEGATIVE writes a bare `\\r` into an open picker, which toggles a\n * multi-select row or submits an N-question review screen irreversibly. So the\n * two bounds that used to make this miss ordinary renders are gone:\n *\n *  - there is NO cap on the distance between consecutive rows. A cap of two\n *    lines was measured to miss a picker whose first option label WRAPPED across\n *    three lines, and a picker with two blank lines between rows. Ascending\n *    digit order across the whole viewport is the structure being looked for;\n *    the gap was never part of it.\n *  - leading decoration is unbounded (`[^0-9A-Za-z]*`) rather than capped at\n *    eight characters, so box drawing plus a selection caret plus deep\n *    indentation cannot push the digit out of reach. The character class still\n *    cannot cross a letter or another digit, so this does not match mid-line\n *    numbers.\n *\n * An agent that printed an ordinary numbered list still matches, and that is\n * still the correct trade.\n */\nexport const PICKER_CHROME_MIN_ROWS = 2;\nconst PICKER_CHROME_ROW = /^[^0-9A-Za-z]*([1-9])[.)\\]:]?\\s+\\S/;\n\nexport function screenShowsPickerChrome(screen: string): boolean {\n\tconst lines = screen.split(\"\\n\");\n\t/** Which digit the run expects next. */\n\tlet expected = 1;\n\tlet run = 0;\n\tfor (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i += 1) {\n\t\tconst line = lines[i];\n\t\tif (line === undefined) continue;\n\t\tconst match = PICKER_CHROME_ROW.exec(line);\n\t\tif (match === null) continue;\n\t\tconst digit = Number(match[1]);\n\t\tif (run > 0 && digit === expected) {\n\t\t\trun += 1;\n\t\t\texpected += 1;\n\t\t\tif (run >= PICKER_CHROME_MIN_ROWS) return true;\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (digit === 1) {\n\t\t\trun = 1;\n\t\t\texpected = 2;\n\t\t\tif (run >= PICKER_CHROME_MIN_ROWS) return true;\n\t\t}\n\t\t// A numbered line that neither continues the run nor starts one is IGNORED,\n\t\t// not treated as the end of the run. Resetting here was a third way to miss\n\t\t// a wrapped label, on top of the gap cap and the decoration cap: a\n\t\t// continuation line reading \"   3 tables and rebuilding every index\" matches\n\t\t// this row pattern with digit 3, and between rows 1 and 2 that reset dropped\n\t\t// the run and the picker went undetected. Skipping instead can only ever\n\t\t// ADD matches, which on this detector is the refusing direction.\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\n/**\n * The WEAK form, for the screens whose layout was never proven: the N-question\n * review screen, the multi-select Submit tab, the open free-text editor. It can\n * only assert that this prompt is still what is on screen. It is used ONLY for\n * `same_prompt` expectations and is reported as `digitMapped: false` so nothing\n * downstream can mistake it for the strong check.\n *\n * (GUARD5-ANCHOR) `anchor_too_weak` is reported separately from `anchor_absent`\n * because the two mean opposite things to a REFUSING caller: \"this prompt is\n * demonstrably not on screen\" versus \"this prompt has no anchor worth\n * searching for, so nothing was demonstrated\". A caller using this to rule a\n * picker OUT must treat `anchor_too_weak` as inconclusive.\n */\nexport function matchPromptStillOnScreen(input: {\n\tscreen: string;\n\titem: QuestionItem;\n}): PickerScreenMatch {\n\tconst prologue = matchPromptOnScreen({\n\t\tscreen: input.screen,\n\t\titem: input.item,\n\t\tdigitMapped: false,\n\t});\n\tif (!prologue.ok) return prologue.failure;\n\t// Deliberately nothing else. The prompt being on screen is the WHOLE claim\n\t// this form makes; the option rows are not consulted at all.\n\treturn { ok: true, reason: \"match\", missing: [], digitMapped: false };\n}\n\nfunction evaluateScreenGuard(input: {\n\tscreen: string;\n\tquestion: PendingQuestion;\n\texpectation: ScreenExpectation;\n\trequireOptionIndex: number | null;\n}): PickerScreenMatch {\n\tconst item = input.question.questions[input.expectation.itemIndex];\n\tif (item === undefined) {\n\t\treturn {\n\t\t\tok: false,\n\t\t\treason: \"anchor_absent\",\n\t\t\tmissing: [`item:${input.expectation.itemIndex}`],\n\t\t\tdigitMapped: input.expectation.kind === \"item_picker\",\n\t\t};\n\t}\n\tif (input.expectation.kind === \"item_picker\") {\n\t\treturn matchPickerScreen({\n\t\t\tscreen: input.screen,\n\t\t\titem,\n\t\t\trequireOptionIndex: input.requireOptionIndex,\n\t\t});\n\t}\n\treturn matchPromptStillOnScreen({ screen: input.screen, item });\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// the guard stack\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nexport interface GuardOutcome {\n\tevaluation: GuardEvaluation;\n\t/**\n\t * The guards that PASSED — the durable audit answer to \"what permitted this\n\t * write\". An abstaining guard is deliberately NOT here: it did not pass, and a\n\t * row saying it did while `evaluation` records `false` for it is a durable\n\t * self-contradiction nobody can later resolve.\n\t */\n\tpassed: AnswerGuardName[];\n\t/**\n\t * (GUARD4-ABSTAIN) Guards that did NOT read positively and were carried\n\t * anyway, because every load-bearing guard had passed and they are in\n\t * `ABSTAINING_GUARDS`. There is no expectation-form condition: the axis\n\t * abstains on weak `same_prompt` keystrokes too, or it aborts proven\n\t * free-text sequences mid-write. Disjoint from `passed`.\n\t */\n\tabstained: AnswerGuardName[];\n\t/** The FIRST guard that failed, in evaluation order. */\n\tfailed: AnswerGuardName | null;\n\tscreenMatch: PickerScreenMatch | null;\n}\n\n/** The attempt-wide guard evidence carried into the ledger, audit and wire. */\nexport interface AttemptEvidence {\n\tguardsPassed: AnswerGuardName[];\n\tguardsAbstained: AnswerGuardName[];\n\tevaluation: GuardEvaluation;\n}\n\n/**\n * Fold one keystroke's `GuardOutcome` into the attempt-wide evidence record.\n *\n * The guards run before EVERY byte and their sources can change between reads\n * (the permission axis is a latch any hook event overwrites), so a record that\n * keeps only the final keystroke's picture can claim \"nothing abstained\" about\n * a sequence whose earlier bytes were permitted only by abstention — a false\n * ledger row, a false audit event, and a falsely-derived replay. So:\n *\n *   - `guardsAbstained` is the UNION across keystrokes: abstaining once is an\n *     attempt-wide fact;\n *   - `guardsPassed` is the INTERSECTION, minus anything that ever abstained —\n *     a guard passed the attempt only if it passed every evaluation;\n *   - `evaluation` ANDs per guard: the raw reading is `true` only if it read\n *     `true` before every byte.\n *\n * Disjointness (`guardsPassed` ∩ `guardsAbstained` = ∅) holds attempt-wide.\n * `previous` is null on the first keystroke.\n */\nexport function mergeAttemptEvidence(\n\tprevious: AttemptEvidence | null,\n\toutcome: GuardOutcome,\n): AttemptEvidence {\n\tconst guardsAbstained =\n\t\tprevious === null\n\t\t\t? [...outcome.abstained]\n\t\t\t: [...new Set([...previous.guardsAbstained, ...outcome.abstained])];\n\tconst guardsPassed = (\n\t\tprevious === null\n\t\t\t? [...outcome.passed]\n\t\t\t: previous.guardsPassed.filter((guard) => outcome.passed.includes(guard))\n\t).filter((guard) => !guardsAbstained.includes(guard));\n\tconst evaluation =\n\t\tprevious === null\n\t\t\t? { ...outcome.evaluation }\n\t\t\t: (Object.fromEntries(\n\t\t\t\t\t(Object.keys(outcome.evaluation) as AnswerGuardName[]).map(\n\t\t\t\t\t\t(guard) => [\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tguard,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tprevious.evaluation[guard] && outcome.evaluation[guard],\n\t\t\t\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t\t\t),\n\t\t\t\t) as GuardEvaluation);\n\treturn { guardsPassed, guardsAbstained, evaluation };\n}\n\n/** A source that throws or answers `null` counts as FALSE. Never \"probably fine\". */\nasync function readGuardSource(\n\tname: AnswerGuardName,\n\tdeps: AnswerDeps,\n\tread: () => Promise<GuardSourceResult>,\n): Promise<boolean> {\n\ttry {\n\t\tconst value = await read();\n\t\tif (value === null) {\n\t\t\tdeps.log({\n\t\t\t\tevent: \"companion.guard.indeterminate\",\n\t\t\t\tguard: name,\n\t\t\t\tresult: false,\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t\treturn false;\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn value;\n\t} catch (error) {\n\t\tdeps.log({\n\t\t\tevent: \"companion.guard.error\",\n\t\t\tguard: name,\n\t\t\tresult: false,\n\t\t\terror: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),\n\t\t});\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n}\n\n/**\n * The reader for a `veto_only` guard, which has DIFFERENT null semantics from\n * every other class and therefore cannot share `readGuardSource`.\n *\n * `GUARD_CLASSES` defines veto_only as \"Absence is a sound veto; presence is\n * never evidence\". Only an AFFIRMATIVE `false` — the source was read and the\n * thing is genuinely absent — may veto. `null` means the source could not be","numLines":260,"startLine":1860,"totalLines":4259}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"db573407-acf3-4613-88d9-f820e7b14f22","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.226","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
